> Species enemies should be un-genocidable, otherwise you could just get rid 
> of them.  They should also be nastier and created more.

Why not make them genocidable? If you're looking at it from a more
D&D-based perspective (what? Nethack Is Not D&D? ah, darn.), racial
enemies should be less things that are there to cause the character
trouble (the RNG already does enough of that, I think!), but rather,
as I said above, a specific class of creatures against which the
Ranger has specialized training to fight. Thus, the Ranger should be
getting bonuses against them; and if he or she wants to genocide the
one monster that he gets a bit of a break on killing, that should be
up to him/her.

> You might want to limit species enemies to things that make sense, i.e. 
> trolls, orcs, kobolds, or something else like that that has rudimentary brain 
> functioning.  It wouldn't make sense if a mind flayer was a species enemy, 
> for instance.

Strictly speaking, there's no real reason one couldn't be specially
trained to fight molds and fungi, even; on the more useful side of
things, it doesn't make particularly bad sense that a Ranger could be
a sort of pest-control agent, specializing in killing insects. Or
maybe the character is particularly effective against quadrupeds.
Making it totally random would preserve some game balance, though-
you're not guaranteed to get a racial enemy that would be "useful".

